  "query_preview": "..\n\nSE Mi remains between a broad area of surface high pressure centered \nin eastern Canada and TN valley low pressure that has a trough \nreaching up into central IN/OH. This is making gusty E-NE wind the \nweather highlight this afternoon. Cool air reinforced by gusts near \n30 mph check temperatures in mid to upper 60s, further limited by \nthe cloud shield reaching up from the Ohio valley low. This system is\nalso grazing the border region with a stray light shower as moisture\ngets wrung out around the north flank of the 500 mb circulation. The\nlow has less influence farther north toward the Tri Cities and Thumb\nwhere full daytime heating is producing generous coverage of shallow\ncumulus within the cool high pressure air mass augmented by Great \nLakes aggregate boundary layer moisture. These clouds fade with \nsunset while the IN/OH circulation drifts toward the mid Appalachians\ntonight, just enough to pull the cloud shield south of the Mi border\nafter midnight. Gradient wind then remains elevated enough to hold a\ntemperature trend similar to last night for lows in the mid 40s to \naround 50 by sunrise Thursday. \n\nSlow evolution of the mid level rex block proceeds during the late \nweek period resulting in the 500 mb ridge component strengthening \nover the Midwest and Great Lakes. Solid agreement among the 23/12Z \ndeterministic models shows this resulting in surface high pressure \nsettling more over the Great Lakes as the potential nor'easter \nmatures off the New England coast. Easterly wind diminishes slightly \nduring the transition Thursday while maintaining an air mass of \nsimilar temperature and cloud character as today. Textbook early \nfall weather for SE Mi then becomes more firmly established by \nFriday with a gradual modification of the cool high pressure air \nmass through the weekend. This allows afternoon temperatures to gain \nground back into the lower 70s with dry weather through Sunday.",
